finding: Finding against the newly bounded claim: 0cff4e74's guard is not exactly the specified plain-data predicate. It checks own symbols, accessors, and __proto__, but never checks descriptor.enumerable or Object.getPrototypeOf(source). Consequently an object with a non-enumerable own data property, a null prototype, or a custom/class prototype can take the spread path instead of the promised baseline path. This is visible directly from the guard; no exotic search or rerun needed. For objects recursively satisfying the stated plain-data predicate, spread followed by the existing recursive loop preserves baseline keys and rounded values, aliases nothing outside opaque userData, and therefore preserves hash inputs. The failure is the claimed path selection, not an independently demonstrated hash regression.
